,"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/a867be58ab6bf1360c60de7108b5e04d64d2b6d3.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":24,"type":"NAT","correct":"","right":2,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>For the circuit shown below:</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/a9a3bd01f8f6506901931780e588d29d092b49ae.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div>the power supplied by the dependent source is _______ W. (Rounded off to nearest integer)</div></div>\n<div><span>NAT</span>","options":[],"nat":{"low":"286","high":"286"},"solution":"<p>286 (285 - 287)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/b178a2ebce47e64596e2df71583e8b25cf1aef5f.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/5e841ec01aa6a12ba3ace8e4d2164e2db727167a.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":25,"type":"MCQ","correct":"C","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>Consider the circuit given in the figure below:</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/18bbffed01b349136e26f05ef2ef79b259c85c2b.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div>The phasor relation between <em>V</em><sub>1</sub>(<em>t</em>), <em>V</em><sub>2</sub>(<em>t</em>) and <em>V<sub>L</sub></em>(<em>t</em>) can be represented as</div>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/9e86eb6900af6570bf91add0895b104410d37b78.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/cbdf1354f4cf4861f12cbab282006a6352250cb0.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/4fece217e784c4d16a2c9349cc536b15669f2499.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/a96032f3535834ab168eec49366962efbeccb9d7.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/f6807fc6e798ed5ae9517939be6d41749c136a3d.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/8800ad1e00ae6a98bbfd9aebdb2797b105d22370.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":26,"type":"NAT","correct":"","right":2,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>A DC voltage of 200 V is applied to series RL circuit at <em>t</em> = 0, which has R = 200 Ω and L = 40 H.<br />\nThe time at which the voltage drop across the inductor is twice the voltage drop across the resistor is __________ msec. (Answer upto two decimal places)</p></div>\n<div><span>NAT</span>","options":[],"nat":{"low":"81.09","high":"81.09"},"solution":"<p>81.09 (81.00 - 82.00)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/58e333947dffcf38a8ad8112fb45237d53b6d321.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":27,"type":"MCQ","correct":"B","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>Consider the circuit shown in the figure below:</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/3353b66bee86df5acd6f880daef24d8cc32b0739.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div>The z-parameter of the above circuit can be given us,</div>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/2de172944ec6064e059849f30d446a832e1b9208.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/cd2e5863ce3c30c83955fa0e1489977130e2f9b2.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/7f4f9123a828528e0d6b614cc2b55aa82b6978cf.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/69fd6742755ad3318b7bcc43ecec5801ab4f856a.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/8aa52ff1498882020796f70cd8f1906f972b4325.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":28,"type":"NAT","correct":"","right":1,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>For a parallel RLC network, <em>L</em> = 0.5 H and C = 50 mF. The value of resistor (R) needed to make the response underdamped with damping factor, α = 4, is ________ Ω. (Answer upto one decimal place)</p></div>\n<div><span>NAT</span>","options":[],"nat":{"low":"2.5","high":"2.5"},"solution":"<p>2.5 (2.0 - 3.0)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/90838a65e65d05107e04bce918898e2847563f97.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":29,"type":"MSQ","correct":"A,C,D","right":1,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>Consider the following statements regarding reciprocity theorem.<br />\nWhich of the given statements is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> To apply reciprocity theorem, unit of response to excitation should be either ohms or mho.</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> If the network is having dependent source, reciprocity theorem can be applied.</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> While applying reciprocity theorem in the circuit, only one independent source is activated.</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> The application of reciprocity theorem is used in antenna.</div>","isCorrect":true}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(a, c, d)<br />\nThe reciprocity theorem states that in any linear and bilateral network or circuit having only one<br />\nindependent source, the ratio of response to excitation (in ohms or mho) is constant even though<br />\nthe source is interchanged from the input terminals to output terminals. The reciprocity theorem<br />\nin antenna theory is useful when using an antenna in both transmitting and receiving modes.</p>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":30,"type":"NAT","correct":"","right":2,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>A 40 μF capacitance is charged to store 0.1 J energy. An uncharged capacitor of 120 μF is connected in parallel with first one through perfectly conducting leads. The resultant energy of the system is given by __________ J. (correct to three decimal places)</p></div>\n<div><span>NAT</span>","options":[],"nat":{"low":"0.025","high":"0.025"},"solution":"<p>0.025 (0.020 - 0.030)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/273e7a19f7a3940f143fbe2cb96020dbbf25fc82.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div> </div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":31,"type":"NAT","correct":"","right":1,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>In the circuit shown below, the switch is closed at <em>t</em> = 0. The steady state voltage across capacitor <em>C</em><sub>2</sub> is _______V.</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/c0f81bc55d0d8fd1eb26bf16a4c8284c76d3e2d4.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div></div>\n<div><span>NAT</span>","options":[],"nat":{"low":"12","high":"12"},"solution":"<p>12 (12 - 12)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/459a8904f074134927e801742d0ab53a7e611c03.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":32,"type":"MCQ","correct":"C","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>Consider the circuit shown in the figure below:</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/925b51131c1dd77f4dc599d471d7d532add2f5f2.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div>The average power absorbed by the 8 Ω resistor is</div>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> 4.07 mW</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> 13.61 mW</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> 36.86 mW</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> 1 W</div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/71622ebee4b14b5b70aaa851788b365cb477166b.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div> </div>\n<div> </div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":33,"type":"MCQ","correct":"C","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>For the circuit shown in the figure below, the Norton’s equivalent across the terminal <em>a</em> and <em>b</em> is represented as</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/a2b521330281853330086d235d5a8cdbb11c17fc.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/4da444c632227691f4546ddd2a4f3caa472d227b.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/81f56bdae6134e95ba5bec4f7b7499e2a524c87b.png\" loading=\"